---
scope: src/cli
alignment: unreviewed
description: The package's terminal commands, typed by a human in a plain terminal outside any agent session, each routed to the one command that does that job.
intent: >
  This module is the package's front door for a human at a plain terminal,
  outside any agent session. It routes the subcommand a person types to the
  one command that handles it, such as installing, syncing, viewing pipeline
  state or browsing the intent tree, and loads only that command, so asking
  for one never pays for the server or the others. Each command does one job
  and tells the human plainly what it did, or why it could not, leaving the
  project untouched when it fails. Adding a command never changes how the
  existing ones behave.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - A person can run any command from a plain terminal with no agent session, agent harness or prior agent setup in place.
    - Running one command loads only that command. It never starts the MCP server or pays the startup cost of any other command.
    - Each command does one job and ends by telling the human, in a few clear lines, what it did.
    - A command that cannot do its job says why in plain language and exits with a failure status, leaving every file it would have written untouched.
    - Adding a new command leaves every existing command's behavior, output and exit status unchanged.
  undesired:
    - A command that only works inside an agent session, or prints agent-shaped output such as tool-result payloads or instructions meant for a model instead of short human-readable lines.
    - Running one command starts the MCP server or loads other commands' work, so a quick command becomes slow or fails because of an unrelated one.
    - A failed command that leaves partial writes behind, or reports success and exits zero when it did not finish its job.
    - A command that finishes silently, or buries what it did under verbose logs, so the human cannot tell what changed.
    - A command whose behavior, output or exit status shifts because another command was added or changed.
---
